Treat a frame named like base's package frame as base in bccompile.c.

The eval harness binds its allowlisted primitives in a frame of its own
that carries the name of base's package frame, so the byte-code compiler
inlines them as it would base's.  cmp.R's isBaseVar() decides two more
things -- whether a break or next inside a conditional compiles to a
jump or to a loop context, and whether calls on literals are
constant-folded -- and compares frames with base by identity, so the
harness rebinds it in the compiler namespace to accept the named frame
(harnesses/eval.c, eval_compiler_setup).

bccompile.c, carried by features/0001, makes the same identity test in
C, out of reach of that rebinding.  Without this, every such loop
compiles with STARTLOOPCNTXT and 1 + 2 is left as an ADD, where cmp.R
under the harness emits a jump and the constant 3; tests/eval.c checks
the loop case.  This makes the C test match the rebound one.

Instrumentation, not a fix, and not for upstream.  It depends on
features/0001 and goes when that does; if features/0001 lands upstream,
this stays for as long as the harness rebinds isBaseVar.

diff --git a/src/main/bccompile.c b/src/main/bccompile.c
--- a/src/main/bccompile.c
+++ b/src/main/bccompile.c
@@ -1892,7 +1892,11 @@
 static int is_base_var(SEXP sym, Cntxt *cntxt)
 {
     InlineInfo ii = get_inline_info(sym, cntxt, FALSE);
-    return ii.found && (ii.frame == R_BaseNamespace || ii.frame == R_BaseEnv);
+    /* Fuzzing instrumentation: the eval harness rebinds cmp.R's isBaseVar
+       to accept its allowlist frame, named like base's package frame
+       (harnesses/eval.c, eval_compiler_setup); this is the same test. */
+    return ii.found && (ii.frame == R_BaseNamespace || ii.frame == R_BaseEnv ||
+			strcmp(pack_frame_name(ii.frame), "base") == 0);
 }
 
 
